Z||000015||Jefrysuko||10-30-2001||09:03 PM||||I thought someone would have come on here and said this by now but I guess not.  There is someway to check for a plugged cat by manifold vacuum.  If I remember right if you hook a gage up and blip the throttle without a plugged cat the vacuum should fall and then rise quickly (rising and falling at about the same rate).  With a plugged cat the vacuum will fall at a faster rate than it can rise back up.  In an extreme case you won't be able to produce the propper vaccum.  If you have a vacuum gage I would try this but if it dosn't work you didn't hear it from me.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000016||Fiero38SC||10-30-2001||09:05 PM||||I have been through this one.  If you can barely feel the exhaust at the tips, yopu deffinitly have a clogged cat.  If you run it too much like that, you WILL cause your exhaust gaskets to blow.  A cat is much easier to fix that the damn exhaust manifold gaskets.<P>Jason||||reg||1
